# Streaming HTTP

Choose a stream only when incremental delivery is part of the contract. <Symbol path="upwell_axum::stream::Ndjson" /> frames one JSON value per newline; <Symbol path="upwell_axum::stream::RawStream" /> forwards unframed `Bytes` chunks as `application/octet-stream`.

## Stream a response as NDJSON

<Example title="Return independently decodable records">

```rust
use futures_core::Stream;
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;

#[dto]
struct AuditEvent {
    id: u64,
    message: String,
}

#[get("/events")]
async fn events(&self) -> Ndjson<impl Stream<Item = AuditEvent>> {
    Ndjson(load_events())
}
```

Each item becomes one JSON line. Keep individual records bounded and avoid embedding literal framing assumptions in the DTO. A consumer can process complete lines without waiting for the whole response.

</Example>

For binary data whose framing belongs to the application format, return `RawStream<S>` where each item is a `Bytes` chunk:

```rust plain
use upwell::axum::bytes::Bytes;

#[get("/archive")]
async fn archive(&self) -> RawStream<impl Stream<Item = Bytes>> {
    RawStream(read_archive_chunks())
}
```

The generated `archive().await?` method yields a stream of `Bytes` chunks through the `RawStream` decoder. Chunk boundaries are transport boundaries, not durable records; the consumer must implement its own framing across chunks.

## Accept a streamed request

One handler may declare one `#[stream]` parameter as the complete request body. Do not combine it with a second body extractor.

```rust plain
#[post("/events")]
async fn ingest(
    &self,
    #[stream] events: impl Stream<Item = AuditEvent>,
) -> StatusCode {
    consume(events).await;
    StatusCode::ACCEPTED
}
```

The generated `#[stream]` request path is concretely NDJSON: it serializes each typed input item as one JSON line, and the server's <Symbol path="upwell_axum::stream::StreamBody" /> decodes those lines. Although `RawStream<()>` implements <Symbol path="upwell_axum::stream::StreamEncode" /> for byte chunks, the committed route macro does not expose a selector that changes generated request framing to raw bytes. Do not describe raw request streaming as supported by `#[stream]`; use an ordinary `Bytes` body or a custom raw Axum extractor/backend workflow when the complete request is binary.

## Use generated native methods carefully

Generated controller clients mirror the method names and exact item types emitted from your controller. A generated streamed-response method returns an outer result for pre-stream status/transport failure, then a decoded item stream. A streamed-request method accepts the emitted stream argument and returns a unary <Symbol path="upwell_axum::client::response::HttpResponse" />.

Do not copy a fabricated client name from prose. Inspect the client emitted for your controller. The stable backend capabilities are:

| Capability | Reqwest | Hyper |
|---|---|---|
| unary request/response | yes | yes |
| streamed response via <Symbol path="upwell_axum::client::streaming::HttpStreaming" /> | yes | yes |
| streamed request via <Symbol path="upwell_axum::client::streaming::HttpClientStreaming" /> | documented by the symbol inventory | also exercised by the committed HTTP fixture at the recorded source revision |

A method whose transport bounds are not met is absent or fails to type-check rather than silently buffering under the wrong contract. The artifact is internally version-skewed: its `HttpClientStreaming` documentation names Reqwest, while the committed fixture at the recorded revision calls the generated request-stream method over Hyper. Check the implementation and tests in the exact crate release you compile before selecting a backend for streamed uploads.

The committed `Api` fixture makes both generated directions concrete:

```rust plain
use futures::{StreamExt, stream};

let mut ticks = client.ticks(4).await?;
while let Some(tick) = ticks.next().await {
    consume_tick(tick);
}

let total = client.collect(stream::iter([1_u64, 2, 3, 4])).await?;
assert_eq!(*total, 10);
```

`ticks` is an NDJSON response stream and `collect` is an NDJSON request stream followed by a unary `HttpResponse<u64>`. These are emitted fixture names; use the method names generated by your controller.

## Error and cancellation behavior

Malformed NDJSON terminates decoding; it is not skipped as a bad record. The validated <Symbol path="upwell_axum::client::streaming::StreamDecode" /> contract logs a transport or frame-decode failure and ends the stream. If mid-stream application errors must be data, declare the server item as `Result<T, E>` so that error is part of the item type.

Pre-stream non-success HTTP statuses become `ClientError::Remote`. Once headers and a successful stream have begun, a later network failure cannot be converted into a new HTTP status.

Dropping the response stream stops local consumption and drops the response body. The server should treat send failure as cancellation and stop producing work. Dropping a streamed request sender prevents future chunks, but already-sent items may have been processed.

## Bound streaming work

Apply independent limits for:

- server-wide request body bytes and request timeout;
- maximum decoded item size;
- maximum item count or total logical bytes;
- idle time between chunks;
- producer queue/backpressure;
- total operation lifetime;
- application cleanup after disconnect.

Set the committed Axum controls at the `[axum]` path:

```toml
[axum]
max_stream_request_bytes = 16777216
max_stream_request_items = 100000
stream_request_timeout_ms = 30000
request_timeout_ms = 30000
```

`max_stream_request_bytes`, `max_stream_request_items`, and `stream_request_timeout_ms` apply specifically to decoded NDJSON request streams. `0` disables each corresponding aggregate limit or stream deadline, except that the fixed per-line NDJSON limit remains. `request_timeout_ms` is the separate whole-handler deadline. Raw streamed responses are outbound and are not governed by these request settings.

<Callout type="warning" title="Streaming is not unlimited memory avoidance">

An unbounded item, queue, decoder line buffer, or application accumulator can still exhaust memory. Bound every layer, including one NDJSON line.

</Callout>

## Troubleshoot streams

- Immediate 415 or decode failure usually indicates a framing/content-type mismatch.
- A stream ending without an error item can be a transport or malformed-frame failure under the validated decoder contract; inspect client logs and server traces.
- A request-stream method missing on one backend can reflect release-specific capability bounds; compare the defining crate's generated method with that release's backend implementation.
- Binary records split unexpectedly means the application treated raw transport chunks as records.
- A server producer continuing after client drop must observe send errors or a cancellation signal.

See <GuideRef slug="axum/http">HTTP controllers</GuideRef> and <GuideRef slug="clients/native/http">native HTTP clients</GuideRef>.
